1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,250
You put a nozzle into your
car. You press a button.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,050
A few seconds later, you've got petrol.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:08,162
Simple.

4
00:00:09,095 --> 00:00:11,045
But where did that petrol come from?

5
00:00:11,795 --> 00:00:14,105
Was it already hiding
inside the crude oil?

6
00:00:14,755 --> 00:00:17,965
Did someone just boil the
oil until petrol came out?

7
00:00:18,731 --> 00:00:23,181
And what about diesel? What happens
if the crude oil is really heavy?

8
00:00:24,034 --> 00:00:26,284
And perhaps the most important question.

9
00:00:27,202 --> 00:00:29,182
If I somehow got my hands on a barrel of

10
00:00:29,262 --> 00:00:32,352
crude oil, could I make
my own petrol at home?

11
00:00:35,022 --> 00:00:39,612
To answer that, we first need to see what
a refinery actually does to crude oil.

12
00:00:40,345 --> 00:00:41,935
So the crude oil arrives.

13
00:00:42,705 --> 00:00:44,435
First, it has to be cleaned up.

14
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:48,335
Water, salts and other
unwanted material are removed.

15
00:00:49,277 --> 00:00:53,747
Then it's heated. And this is where
the classic refinery image appears.

16
00:00:54,697 --> 00:00:56,607
The enormous distillation column.

17
00:00:57,419 --> 00:00:59,379
It looks like a giant industrial tower.

18
00:00:59,459 --> 00:01:03,449
And essentially, it is a
giant separation machine.

19
00:01:04,344 --> 00:01:06,714
But before we climb into it, one number.

20
00:01:07,491 --> 00:01:10,661
A barrel of crude oil is a
hundred and fifty-nine litres.

21
00:01:10,811 --> 00:01:14,941
American refineries pour in a
hundred and fifty-nine litres...

22
00:01:15,811 --> 00:01:18,241
and get out about a hundred and seventy.

23
00:01:18,691 --> 00:01:20,481
More comes out than went in.

24
00:01:22,815 --> 00:01:24,165
Nobody is cheating.

25
00:01:24,575 --> 00:01:28,105
The products are simply less dense
than the crude they came from.

26
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:32,271
A refinery is not a machine
that takes petrol out of oil.

27
00:01:33,121 --> 00:01:35,631
It's a machine that makes
new things out of it.

28
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:40,150
And to see how, we have to look at
what is actually inside that barrel.

29
00:01:40,899 --> 00:01:43,479
Crude oil is not a
liquid version of petrol.

30
00:01:43,559 --> 00:01:46,189
And it isn't one chemical compound.

31
00:01:47,183 --> 00:01:49,913
It's a mixture of thousands
of different hydrocarbons,

32
00:01:50,243 --> 00:01:53,433
along with sulphur,
nitrogen, oxygen and metals.

33
00:01:54,341 --> 00:01:58,451
And they come in wildly
different sizes. Some are small.

34
00:01:59,041 --> 00:02:02,311
Some are so large and heavy
that they behave more like tar.

35
00:02:03,102 --> 00:02:06,622
So when we say oil, we're talking
about an enormous molecular soup.

36
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:09,432
And not all of that soup is the same.

37
00:02:10,195 --> 00:02:12,725
Crude oil is described
using two characteristics.

38
00:02:14,095 --> 00:02:15,765
One is light versus heavy.

39
00:02:16,295 --> 00:02:19,525
Light crude contains more
small hydrocarbon molecules.

40
00:02:20,475 --> 00:02:23,325
Heavy crude contains
more large, complex ones.

41
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:26,010
The second is sweet versus sour.

42
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:28,350
That has nothing to do with taste.

43
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:31,450
Sweet crude contains little sulphur.

44
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:33,750
Sour crude contains more.

45
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:36,070
So two barrels can look similar...

46
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:38,470
while being very different raw materials.

47
00:02:39,615 --> 00:02:43,345
Which is one reason why refineries
are not all built the same way.

48
00:02:45,475 --> 00:02:50,565
Back to the column. The refinery
isn't separating molecules one by one.

49
00:02:51,269 --> 00:02:55,479
It's separating groups of hydrocarbons
according to their boiling behaviour.

50
00:02:56,357 --> 00:03:00,187
The crude is heated to around three
hundred and fifty degrees Celsius.

51
00:03:00,827 --> 00:03:04,687
Hot vapour rises up the column
while cooler liquid flows downward.

52
00:03:05,522 --> 00:03:08,292
Different parts of the mixture
condense at different levels.

53
00:03:09,002 --> 00:03:13,032
Near the top, the lightest
hydrocarbons. Gases.

54
00:03:14,122 --> 00:03:17,492
Below them, naphtha, boiling up to
about a hundred and ninety degrees.

55
00:03:18,445 --> 00:03:23,205
Then kerosene, roughly a hundred
and ninety to two hundred and fifty.

56
00:03:23,285 --> 00:03:25,705
Then the diesel range, very roughly two

57
00:03:25,785 --> 00:03:28,696
hundred and fifty to
three hundred and fifty.

58
00:03:29,146 --> 00:03:32,496
And at the bottom, the stuff that
really doesn't want to evaporate.

59
00:03:33,086 --> 00:03:34,736
Heavy oil and residue.

60
00:03:35,586 --> 00:03:38,956
Those numbers are deliberately vague,
and that isn't me being careless.

61
00:03:39,849 --> 00:03:42,229
The fractions overlap,
and where one ends and the

62
00:03:42,309 --> 00:03:44,919
next begins is something
the refinery chooses.

63
00:03:45,749 --> 00:03:48,179
It's a setting, not a law of nature.

64
00:03:50,619 --> 00:03:53,609
And at this point, you might think. Great.

65
00:03:54,059 --> 00:03:56,229
We've separated the
petrol from the diesel.

66
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:58,470
But... not really.

67
00:04:00,688 --> 00:04:03,078
Here's the big secret of modern refining.

68
00:04:04,088 --> 00:04:08,598
A refinery doesn't just separate
hydrocarbons. It changes them.

69
00:04:09,257 --> 00:04:10,907
Here's why it has to.

70
00:04:11,743 --> 00:04:14,653
Take a good, light crude
and simply distil it.

71
00:04:15,483 --> 00:04:18,773
About twenty per cent of the barrel
comes off in the petrol range.

72
00:04:19,649 --> 00:04:21,471
Twenty per cent.

73
00:04:22,921 --> 00:04:26,711
Meanwhile, petrol is close to half
of everything a refinery sells.

74
00:04:26,841 --> 00:04:28,591
And it gets worse.

75
00:04:29,581 --> 00:04:33,011
That straight-run petrol has an
octane rating of about fifty.

76
00:04:33,858 --> 00:04:35,058
Your car wants ninety-five.

77
00:04:36,843 --> 00:04:40,513
Too little petrol. And what
there is, knocks too easily.

78
00:04:41,203 --> 00:04:43,493
Both gaps have to be closed by chemistry.

79
00:04:44,571 --> 00:04:49,201
First, quantity. We can break
large molecules into smaller ones.

80
00:04:49,731 --> 00:04:50,961
This is called cracking.

81
00:04:51,882 --> 00:04:53,932
And the name is wonderfully literal.

82
00:04:54,802 --> 00:04:58,852
Large hydrocarbon molecules are
chemically broken into smaller molecules.

83
00:04:59,822 --> 00:05:02,172
One important method
is catalytic cracking.

84
00:05:03,075 --> 00:05:05,555
Another is hydrocracking,
which uses hydrogen

85
00:05:05,635 --> 00:05:08,485
under high pressure
together with catalysts.

86
00:05:08,815 --> 00:05:12,665
This matters most when the refinery
is working with heavy crude.

87
00:05:13,456 --> 00:05:17,186
Suddenly those enormous molecules
aren't something to be sold off cheaply.

88
00:05:17,996 --> 00:05:20,086
They're raw material for petrol.

89
00:05:20,176 --> 00:05:22,766
So the refinery isn't just asking...

90
00:05:23,516 --> 00:05:25,266
what's already in this crude oil?

91
00:05:26,002 --> 00:05:28,232
It's asking... what can we turn it into?

92
00:05:30,272 --> 00:05:32,262
Now for the second gap. Quality.

93
00:05:33,312 --> 00:05:34,942
Petrol isn't one chemical.

94
00:05:35,872 --> 00:05:38,682
It's blended from several
different refinery streams.

95
00:05:39,572 --> 00:05:41,772
Some hydrocarbons are
reformed into molecules

96
00:05:41,852 --> 00:05:44,422
with better properties for petrol engines.

97
00:05:45,359 --> 00:05:48,779
Others are rearranged by
processes such as isomerisation.

98
00:05:48,899 --> 00:05:52,009
And cracked products contribute as well.

99
00:05:52,729 --> 00:05:55,499
The result is a carefully
controlled mixture.

100
00:05:55,709 --> 00:05:59,339
And that octane number measures
its resistance to knocking.

101
00:06:00,209 --> 00:06:02,279
The scale is beautifully arbitrary.

102
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:06,721
One particular molecule,
heptane, was defined as zero.

103
00:06:07,731 --> 00:06:10,601
Another, iso-octane, was
defined as a hundred.

104
00:06:11,171 --> 00:06:13,741
Everything else is
measured against those two.

105
00:06:14,485 --> 00:06:17,605
So when you see ninety-five
at a filling station, that

106
00:06:17,685 --> 00:06:20,775
number isn't telling you how
much energy is in the fuel.

107
00:06:21,599 --> 00:06:25,469
It's telling you how well it resists
igniting before it is supposed to.

108
00:06:26,133 --> 00:06:29,233
The petrol in your tank is therefore
the result of a whole chain of

109
00:06:29,313 --> 00:06:33,603
physical separation, chemical
transformation and precise blending.

110
00:06:35,833 --> 00:06:37,843
Diesel follows the same story.

111
00:06:38,553 --> 00:06:40,683
Some of it comes straight
from distillation.

112
00:06:41,593 --> 00:06:43,963
The rest is built by the
same kind of chemistry.

113
00:06:45,021 --> 00:06:47,111
And diesel has its own requirements.

114
00:06:48,021 --> 00:06:52,491
Its ignition quality is measured by the
cetane number, not the octane number.

115
00:06:53,161 --> 00:06:56,451
So diesel isn't simply the stuff
that's heavier than petrol.

116
00:06:57,341 --> 00:06:59,531
It's a deliberately manufactured fuel.

117
00:07:00,363 --> 00:07:03,903
There's another problem.
Crude oil contains sulphur.

118
00:07:03,983 --> 00:07:08,013
And we don't want large amounts
of it ending up in modern fuels.

119
00:07:08,997 --> 00:07:12,807
So refineries run processes
such as hydrotreating.

120
00:07:13,497 --> 00:07:16,407
Hydrogen reacts with the
sulphur-containing molecules.

121
00:07:17,393 --> 00:07:19,523
And here's a nice refinery trick.

122
00:07:20,353 --> 00:07:22,143
The sulphur doesn't get thrown away.

123
00:07:22,893 --> 00:07:26,243
It comes out the other end
as solid, elemental sulphur.

124
00:07:26,859 --> 00:07:31,329
Something that started as an undesirable
contaminant gets sold as a product.

125
00:07:33,669 --> 00:07:37,019
So what actually comes out of that
hundred and fifty-nine litres?

126
00:07:37,967 --> 00:07:41,127
In American refineries, roughly
seventy-four litres of petrol.

127
00:07:41,207 --> 00:07:44,657
Forty-seven of diesel and heating oil.

128
00:07:45,247 --> 00:07:47,017
Seventeen of jet fuel.

129
00:07:47,817 --> 00:07:52,917
And then the rest. Petroleum coke,
refinery gas, bitumen, lubricants,

130
00:07:52,997 --> 00:07:55,727
feedstocks for the chemical
industry, and sulphur.

131
00:07:56,341 --> 00:07:57,681
Which is why saying...

132
00:07:57,761 --> 00:08:01,991
one barrel gives you so many
litres of petrol... is misleading.

133
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:05,170
A refinery isn't a petrol factory.

134
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:08,650
It's a chemical factory
making many things at once.

135
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:14,010
And now we arrive at the
question we started with.

136
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:16,850
Suppose you had a barrel of crude oil.

137
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:18,990
Could you make your own petrol?

138
00:08:19,876 --> 00:08:21,426
In principle... yes.

139
00:08:22,284 --> 00:08:24,774
Nothing about it violates
the laws of physics.

140
00:08:25,784 --> 00:08:27,274
You can heat crude oil.

141
00:08:27,804 --> 00:08:30,254
You can separate fractions
by boiling point.

142
00:08:30,864 --> 00:08:32,854
You can transform hydrocarbons.

143
00:08:33,650 --> 00:08:37,820
But there's a rather large problem.
Actually... There are several.

144
00:08:38,633 --> 00:08:42,253
You might think. I'll just build
a still. Heat the crude oil.

145
00:08:42,333 --> 00:08:46,223
Collect whatever boils off
in the petrol range. Done.

146
00:08:46,953 --> 00:08:48,643
But you already know what you'd get.

147
00:08:49,193 --> 00:08:51,704
About a fifth of the
barrel, at octane fifty.

148
00:08:52,154 --> 00:08:54,044
You haven't removed the sulphur.

149
00:08:54,614 --> 00:08:58,204
You haven't produced a fuel that
behaves correctly in a modern engine.

150
00:08:59,057 --> 00:09:01,907
So simple distillation
gets you part of the way.

151
00:09:02,017 --> 00:09:04,147
But only part of the way.

152
00:09:04,937 --> 00:09:07,027
Fine. I'll build a real refinery.

153
00:09:08,019 --> 00:09:09,599
Okay. Let's add catalytic cracking.

154
00:09:09,679 --> 00:09:13,089
And hydrocracking. And
reforming. And isomerisation.

155
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:14,999
And hydrotreating.

156
00:09:15,619 --> 00:09:16,759
Let's add hydrogen production.

157
00:09:16,839 --> 00:09:18,599
High-pressure equipment.

158
00:09:18,679 --> 00:09:20,089
Catalysts.

159
00:09:20,685 --> 00:09:23,795
Heat exchangers. Distillation columns.

160
00:09:23,925 --> 00:09:28,755
Compressors. Pumps. Control systems.
Storage tanks. Safety systems.

161
00:09:28,845 --> 00:09:30,365
Waste treatment.

162
00:09:30,445 --> 00:09:34,155
And laboratories to continuously
analyse the products.

163
00:09:35,103 --> 00:09:39,013
Congratulations. You have just
started building... a refinery.

164
00:09:41,763 --> 00:09:45,633
And suddenly the reason refineries
are enormous becomes obvious.

165
00:09:46,243 --> 00:09:48,463
They aren't enormous
because someone couldn't

166
00:09:48,543 --> 00:09:50,813
find a sufficiently large cooking pot.

167
00:09:51,848 --> 00:09:54,738
They're enormous because that
is what modern fuel takes.

168
00:09:55,568 --> 00:09:58,158
And here's the part I
find most fascinating.

169
00:09:59,228 --> 00:10:02,918
When you put petrol into your car,
you aren't putting crude oil into it.

170
00:10:03,348 --> 00:10:06,638
You aren't even putting one
particular substance into it.

171
00:10:07,295 --> 00:10:10,705
You're putting a carefully
engineered mixture of hydrocarbons.

172
00:10:11,255 --> 00:10:15,585
Some molecules may have started as
light components of the original crude.

173
00:10:16,417 --> 00:10:19,527
Others were created by breaking
larger molecules apart.

174
00:10:20,377 --> 00:10:22,687
Others were chemically rearranged.

175
00:10:23,337 --> 00:10:27,187
So the petrol in your tank is not
something that was found inside the Earth.

176
00:10:27,930 --> 00:10:30,740
It is something that was
manufactured from crude oil.

177
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:34,910
Not liquid dinosaur juice.

178
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,850
Not simply distilled crude oil.

179
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:40,880
But the end product of one
of the most complicated

180
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:43,690
industrial processes
humans have developed.

181
00:10:44,791 --> 00:10:46,531
All so that, eventually,
you can put a nozzle

182
00:10:46,611 --> 00:10:49,341
into your car, press a
button, and drive away.

183
00:10:51,901 --> 00:10:54,091
Not bad for a barrel of black goo.
